The constructor-level pinning check canonicalized both terms through K’s parser and obtained identical KAST hashes; true and false satisfying witnesses agree with both Python implementations. Changing the actual executed body from “remainder == 0” to “remainder == 1” made the original true example get stuck with result `false`, so the theorem is body-sensitive. Static review has also found a material generated-semantics mismatch: on a canonical 5,000-digit valid fraction, CPython 3.10 raises its 4,300-digit conversion limit, while the rebuilt K semantics terminates with `true`. I’m preserving that concrete witness and now running the separate false-postcondition non-vacuity test.
